## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-protocol data governance: masking at the boundary across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC, driven by per-tenant OpenAPI contracts. It tests how to derive and enforce field visibility, roll out changes safely, and audit policy drift.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant field-level visibility across protocols
- Contract-driven masking rules derived from OpenAPI
- Central registry (Redis) for tenant/resource visibility
- Canary rollouts and rollout safety across services

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskVisible(obj, tenantId, contract) {
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(v => maskVisible(v, tenantId, contract));
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  const out = {};
  const allowed = contract?.visibleFields || [];
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
    out[k] = allowed.includes(k) ? v : 'REDACTED';
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift and automated rollback when masking changes break clients?
- What metrics would you collect to detect masking-related regressions across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world setups use multiple IdPs; token replay and cross-provider binding are critical issues.

## Key Concepts
- JWKS caching and key rotation per provider
- Replay protection with jti in distributed cache
- Token binding via CNF and mTLS
- Revocation propagation and token lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
function verifyJwt(token, provider) {
  const key = getJwksKey(provider, token.kid);
  const payload = jwtVerify(token, key);
  if (Date.now() >= payload.exp * 1000) throw new Error('expired');
  if (redis.exists(`jti:${payload.jti}`)) throw new Error('replay');
  redis.set(`jti:${payload.jti}`, 1, 'EX', payload.exp - now);
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle JWKS rotation events?
- How would you monitor and debug cross-provider revocation flows?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design scalable, resilient multi-region systems with per-tenant isolation, hot-tenant mitigation, and cost control—core competencies for distributed systems engineers at senior levels.

## Key Concepts

- Time-based shard design and tenant isolation strategies
- Multi-region replication with quorum-based consistency
- Hot-tenant mitigation and dynamic shard rebalancing
- Data lifecycle management: tiered storage, retention, compaction, and archival

## Code Example

```javascript
function shardKey(tenantId, timestamp) {
  const hourBucket = Math.floor(timestamp / 3600000);
  return `${tenantId}:${hourBucket}`;
}

class TimeSeriesStore {
  constructor(regions, replicationFactor = 3) {
    this.regions = regions;
    this.replicationFactor = replicationFactor;
    this.quorum = Math.floor(replicationFactor / 2) + 1;
  }

  async write(tenantId, timestamp, data) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const writes = replicas.map(region => this.writeToRegion(region, key, data));
    await Promise.all(writes);
    return this.verifyQuorum(writes);
  }

  selectReplicas(shardKey) {
    return this.regions.slice(0, this.replicationFactor);
  }

  async read(tenantId, timestamp) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const reads = replicas.map(region => this.readFromRegion(region, key));
    return this.mergeResults(await Promise.all(reads));
  }
}
```

